{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ESNext",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    // Mirrors web/tsconfig. This config has no `outDir`, so a bare
    // `tsc -p tsconfig.json` emits a `.js` twin beside EVERY source file under
    // `src/` and `scripts/`. That is exactly how ~80 stale compiled twins
    // accumulated — including 13 `*.test.js` copies that `bun test` collected
    // as duplicate suites (which made `todo.test.ts` fail on the shared test
    // database and doubled the reported test counts). Nothing needs tsc to
    // emit: the backend bundles with `bun build`, the web app builds with vite,
    // and `typecheck` already passes `--noEmit` explicitly.
    "noEmit": true,
    // Strict null checking, enabled 2026-10-11.
    //
    // The two halves are separate decisions and both matter:
    //
    //   "strict": true              the whole family (null, implicit any, etc.)
    //   "strictNullChecks": true    restated explicitly because it is the one
    //                               that changes what the compiler PROVES.
    //
    // Without strictNullChecks TypeScript does not narrow a discriminated
    // union at all, which is why `chat/types.ts` needed `?: never` markers to
    // make `PhaseResult` readable. With it on, the phase contract is enforced
    // rather than documented, and the 19 "possibly null" sites this surfaced
    // are real: each one is a value read before anything proved it exists.
    //
    // Enabling this was a deliberate, measured step: 57 errors across 17 files,
    // all now fixed. Two of them were genuine latent bugs rather than type
    // noise - TS2783 "specified more than once, so this usage will be
    // overwritten" in `services/mcp/manager.ts` and `services/scheduler/
    // schedulerExecution.ts` - i.e. object literals where a field was
    // assigned twice and the first value was silently discarded.
    "strict": true,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    // Matches web/tsconfig: declaration files (node_modules) are the
    // packages' contract, not our source. A dependency minor bump otherwise
    // breaks the build on third-party .d.ts drift in code paths we never
    // import (e.g. assistant-ui cloud adapters). Our own source stays fully
    // checked.
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["bun"],
    // No `baseUrl`. It was deprecated in TypeScript 6.0 and removed in 7.0.
    // `paths` entries resolve relative to the tsconfig file that declares them
    // when `baseUrl` is absent, and the single entry below already does.
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src", "scripts"]
}
